  "transcript": "# Proposals for backbench debates\n\n## Pushing for a debate on cancer research\n> MPs want a Chamber debate to press the Government to speed up clinical trials, protect research investment, and meet survival targets.\n\nChair: Our first application is from Scott Arthur for a Chamber debate on the future of cancer research in the UK; I declare my support for Cancer Research UK and chair the all‑party group on smoking and health.\nDr Arthur: This debate should push the Government and spotlight both health and economic benefits of faster cancer research—trials are delayed and promising treatments reach patients slowly.\nDr Arthur: A constituent, a clinician who won £70 million to research glioblastoma, says the work would have started a year earlier in the US because of UK delays—an example of the problem.\nDr Arthur: Universities want about £500 million for science infrastructure; industry warned that intellectual property might end up overseas if doing the work abroad is more attractive.\nDr Arthur: This is grassroots: MPs supporting the debate did so after constituents affected by cancer raised the issue, not because we pushed them to sign.\nClive Jones: Cancer affects one in two of us and remains the leading cause of death; survival rates improved since the 1970s but progress is slowing and the UK could be doing more.\nClive Jones: The Government's national cancer plan aims for three‑in‑four five‑year survival by 2035, but to reach it we must improve research and treatments—hence the debate.\nMary Glindon: My late husband benefited from being on a trial; trials make a real difference to patients.\nChair: November includes awareness months for some cancers, which could make timing useful, though scheduling in the Chamber is tight because of the Budget and a short recess.\n\n\n## Illegal e-bikes, conversion kits and public safety\n> MPs want a national debate about illegal e-bikes and kits because of crime, speeding, fires, and damage to legitimate businesses.\n\nChair: Next is Dr Arthur's second application on the impact of illegal e-bikes and conversion kits on communities.\nDr Arthur: This is a big local issue—police and constituents raise it constantly. Legal e-bikes are great, but illegal ones and conversion kits cause disruption and danger.\nDr Arthur: The London Fire Brigade told me they see a battery fire every two days and even confiscated bikes catch fire in depots; emergency services are concerned by government inaction.\nMs Minns: The issue affects all constituencies and the legal market. Only 9% of bikes sold in the UK are e-bikes, versus much higher rates in Germany and the Netherlands—illegal sales may be a factor.\nMs Minns: Illegal kits undercut legitimate businesses and have hit low‑powered moped sales; why buy a licensed, insured moped when an illegal kit gives the same speed?\nMs Minns: If an e‑bike is illegal, it's treated as a motor vehicle—so the Motor Insurers' Bureau has seen a 73% increase in claims where people were injured by illegal e‑bikes.\nMs Minns: China has banned the sale of these kits; much of the illegal stock comes from abroad. With the Tour de France coming to parts of the UK next year, it's timely to act.\nMr Dillon: I had a lithium battery fire in my constituency that closed the Paddington line. Would you take a Westminster Hall debate sooner if Chamber time wasn't available?\nMs Minns: We prefer the Chamber because it's a national issue, but if Westminster Hall gets an earlier slot we'd be happy to take it to press the Government for action.\nChair: A Westminster Hall debate is more likely to come sooner; Clerks will be in touch about scheduling.\n\n\n## Commemorating the Jarrow Crusade\n> A debate to mark the 90th anniversary of the Jarrow March would reflect on unemployment, the dignity of work, and regional inequality.\n\nChair: The final application is for a debate on the legacy of the Jarrow Crusade, 90 years since the 1936 march; Olivia Bailey is presenting for Kate Osborne.\nOlivia Bailey: Kate Osborne wants Parliament to mark the march when 200 men walked nearly 300 miles to protest unemployment and industrial decline, and to reflect on its national significance.\nOlivia Bailey: Its legacy ties to dignity of work, regional inequality, and how Parliament hears industrial communities—an anniversary that matters beyond Jarrow itself.\nMartin Vickers: The application names the Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade to respond; last time the Department for Work and Pensions did. Any reason for the change?\nOlivia Bailey: I'm not sure; perhaps it's linked to recent workers' rights legislation and the Employment Rights Act 2025, which may make DBIST appropriate.\nChair: We can offer 22 October as the debate date to match Kate's request; Olivia, can you confirm quickly if that's acceptable?\nOlivia Bailey: I can provisionally accept and will check Kate's diary immediately; if she can't make it, I offered to lead the debate instead so it wouldn't slip until November.\nChair: Thank you. Clerks will contact you formally about timing and any follow‑up correspondence.\n\n\n## Committee process and next steps\n> The Committee encouraged flexibility on timing, warned of limited Chamber slots, and said Clerks would confirm dates.\n\nChair: We have limited Chamber time because of the Budget and other business; we will try to find suitable Chamber slots but may offer Westminster Hall sooner.\nDr Arthur: Timely debates would help hold the Government to account and push for concrete steps on trials, imports, and safety—constituents expect action.\nClive Jones: Cross‑party support exists for the cancer debate and the e‑bike debate; I hope that shows broad parliamentary interest.\nChair: That concludes public proceedings. The Committee will now consider the applications in private and allocate time accordingly.\n",
